Sylow’s theorems
If G is a ﬁnite group of order pkn, where p is prime and n is not divisible by
p, then the Sylow theorems state that
1. G has subgroups of order pk;
2. these Sylow p-subgroups are all conjugate; and
3. the number sp of Sylow p-subgroups satisﬁes sp ≡ 1 mod p. (Note also
that, by the orbit-stabilizer theorem, sp is a divisor of n).
To prove the ﬁrst statement, let G act by right multiplication on all subsets
of G of size pk: since the number of these subsets is not divisible by p, there
is a stabilizer of order divisible by pk, and therefore equal to pk. To prove
the second statement, and also to prove that any p-subgroup is contained in
a Sylow p-subgroup, let any p-subgroup Q act on the right cosets Pg of any
