3.3.8 Covering groups
It turns out that, in general, the special linear group SL n(q) is the full covering
group (see Section 2.7) of the simple group PSL n(q). There are however a few
exceptions. The exceptional part of the Schur multiplier is always a p-group,
where p is the characteristic of the deﬁning ﬁeld of the group.
In fact, most of the exceptions arise as a result of the exceptional iso-
morphisms above. Thus A5 has a double cover, and therefore so does L 2(4);
similarly,A6 has a triple cover, and therefore so does L 2(9). Both L 2(7) and
A8 have double covers, and therefore so do L 3(2) and L 4(2). The only other
exceptional cover of a simple group PSL n(q) is 4 2.L3(4). (See Section 3.12 for
a sketch of a construction of this group.)
3.4 Bilinear, sesquilinear and quadratic forms
There are a number of useful inner products on real and complex vector
spaces, and these inner products give rise to various bilinear, sesquilinear and
quadratic forms. The situation is similar for vector spaces over ﬁnite ﬁelds, al-
