4.8.3 Simplicity
To prove simplicity of 3D4(q) we use an easy generalisation of Iwasawa’s
Lemma given in Exercise ?? We use the action of 3D4(q) on the isotropic
1-spaces ⟨v⟩ with v ∗v = 0. The point stabilizer has orbits of lengths 1,
q3(q + 1), q7(q + 1) and q11 on the points, and it is easy to check that none
of the putative block sizes 1 + q3 +q4, 1 + q7 +q8 or 1 + q3 +q4 +q7 +q8
divides the total number of points, so the action is primitive. The point sta-
bilizer is q2+3+6:SL2(q).Cq3−1, and it has a normal soluble subgroup q2+3+6
which is contained in the derived subgroup. Let H be the subgroup of 3D4(q)
generated by the conjugates of q2+3+6: it remains to show that H = 3D4(q).
SinceE(λ) is conjugate to A(λ), which is in q2+3+6,H containsq2+3+6:SL2(q).
Similarly,H containsq1+8:SL2(q3), which is generated by conjugates of C(λ).
Therefore H strictly contains the point stabilizer q2+3+6:SL2(q).Cq3−1. But
the latter subgroup is maximal, since the action on its cosets is primitive, and
therefore H = 3D4(q). We conclude that 3D4(q) is simple for all q.
There are no proper covers of any of the groups 3D4(q). The only auto-
morphisms are ﬁeld automorphisms, of the ﬁeld of order q3.
