Further reading
Much of the material in this chapter is not available in book form anywhere,
certainly not in one place. For the standard treatment of exceptional groups
via Lie algebras, see Carter [13]. This book is essential reading for anyone
seriously interested in ﬁnite simple groups.
A more abstract treatment of octonions, Jordan algebras and exceptional
groups is given by Springer and Veldkamp [94], who deal thoroughly with the
groups of type G2, 3D4, F4, E6 and 2E6. They do not cover the exceptional
characteristics 2 and 3, nor do they describe E7 or E8, or the Suzuki or Ree
groups. A detailed description of rings of ‘integral’ octonions (in character-
istic 0) may be found in the recent book by Conway and Smith [21]. For an
amazingly thorough treatment of Jordan algebras in all their glory, I recom-
mend the immensely readable book ‘A taste of Jordan algebras’ by Kevin
McCrimmon [80].
The Suzuki groups are treated in a number of more general texts, for
example Huppert and Blackburn [52] (which also includes a discussion of the
small Ree groups, but without proofs), and Don Taylor’s book on classical
groups [96], as well as the book by L¨ uneburg [74]. More details of my approach
to the Ree groups may be found in [105].
