5.7 The Fischer groups
The discovery of these groups arose out of the observation that the symmetric
groups are generated by the conjugacy class of transpositions, which has the
property that the product of any two transpositions has order 1, 2 or 3.
One might be tempted to ask whether there are any other groups with
this property. The answer is yes: for example, the Weyl groups of types Dn
and En. More generally, the orthogonal groups O ±
2n(2) have a conjugacy class
of orthogonal transvections such that any two transvections either commute
or generate O −
2 (2) ∼=S3. Similarly, the unitary groups over F4 have a class of
unitary transvections, such that any two such transvections either commute
or generate SU 2(2) ∼= S3. Similarly the symplectic transvections in Sp 2n(2)
either commute or generate Sp 2(2) ∼=S3.
For technical reasons to do with the inductive classiﬁcation of such groups,
Fischer [38, 39] deﬁned a 3 -transposition group to be a ﬁnite group G gener-
