and the embedding of 2 .Fi22 in 2 2.2E6(2) suggested to Fischer that perhaps
there were bigger simple groups B and M with 2 2.2E6(2) < 2.B < M. They
would not be 3-transposition groups, but 2E6(2) contains a class of involu-
tions whose products have order at most 4, so this was considered a suitable
generalization. Indeed, M contains a class of 6-transpositions, i.e. involutions
whose products have order at most 6.
These groups, now known as the Baby Monster and the Monster, were
eventually proved to exist. The Monster turned out to contain a number of
other interesting subgroups, such as 3 .Fi24, and three more sporadic groups,
namely the Held group He (previously known, and contained in Fi 24—though
this fact was not known until the discovery of the Monster), the Harada–
Norton group HN, and the Thompson group Th, centralizing elements of
orders 7, 5 and 3 respectively.
5.8.1 The Monster
The Monster has a representation in dimension 196883 which has a kind of
non-associative algebra structure on it. By adjoining an identity element we
