5.9.4 The O’Nan group
By the time we reach the O’Nan group, all pretence that we could do the cal-
culations by hand has to be abandoned. The group has order 460 815 505 920 =
29.34.5.73.11.19.31, and the smallest matrix representation is a representation
of the triple cover 3 .O’N in 45 dimensions over F7. Generating matrices were
obtained by Richard Parker, and proved to generate the required group by
Alex Ryba (see [88]). This existence proof relies on the fact that this repre-
sentation supports an invariant skew-symmetric trilinear form. There is an
outer automorphism of 3 .O’N which interchanges this representation with
its dual, and extends the group to 3 .O’N:2. The trilinear form gives rise to
an anti-commutative, non-associative algebra structure on the underlying 90-
dimensional space.
The original construction by Sims, however, was as a group of permuta-
tions on 122760 points, with point stabilizer L 3(7):2. Again, the outer auto-
morphism of O’N interchanges two such permutation representations. We can
therefore express the group O’N:2 as a group of automorphisms of a bipartite
graph on 2 × 122760 points. In fact we may choose this graph to be regular
of degree 456, and O’N:2 is the full automorphism group of this graph.
The centralizer in O’N of a non-inner automorphism of order 2 turns out
to be the Janko group J 1.
The maximal subgroups of O’N are listed in Table 5.11.
There are a number of other interesting representations of the O’Nan group
and its triple cover, for example a 154-dimensional representation of O’N
over F3, and a 153-dimensional representation of 3 .O’N over F4. The smallest
representations in characteristic 0 however have dimension 342 (for the triple
cover 3.O’N) or 10944 (for the simple group O’N).
5.9.5 The Lyons group
The Lyons group was discovered as a result of classifying simple groups with
an involution centralizer 2 .An (see Section 2.7.2). The smallest value of n for
which 2 .An has non-central involutions (a necessary requirement for being
an involution centraliser in a ﬁnite simple group) is n = 8, and indeed the
McLaughlin group has an involution centralizer 2 .A8. The only other case
which arises is n = 11, in the Lyons group Ly. Moreover, a 3-cycle in 2 .A11
centralizes 2.A8, and it turns out that the full centralizer of this 3-cycle in the
Lyons group is the triple cover 3 .McL of the McLaughlin group.
Hence the normalizer of this group of order 3 is 3 .McL:2, which contains
S3 × M11. This suggests that Ly can be generated by subgroups 2 .A11 and
S3 × M11 intersecting in 2 × M11, and indeed this turns out to be the case.
Richard Parker constructed the Lyons group in this way as a group of 111 ×111
matrices over F5, but his construction was not the ﬁrst. That honour goes
to Sims, who eﬀectively constructed the group as a permutation group on
8835156 points.
