For further information about the sporadic simple groups, the Atlas of Fi-
nite Groups [18] is an indispensible reference. Griess’s book ‘Twelve sporadic
--- PHYSICAL PAGE 244 ---
230 5 Sporadic groups
groups’ [43] is the only book on this subject which is written at an introduc-
tory level, and covers those groups which are visible inside Conway’s group.
His book (like mine) becomes more and more sketchy towards the end, and
much is left as (very instructive) exercises for the reader. Conway and Sloane’s
‘Sphere packings, lattices and groups’ [20] contains several chapters of interest,
particularly covering M 24, the Leech lattice, and the Monster.
Aschbacher’s book ‘Sporadic groups’ [1] is written from the point of view of
collecting information needed for the proof of CFSG. It contains a great deal
of carefully selected information about some of the sporadic groups, but is not
easy to read. His other book ‘3-transposition groups’ [2] is in the same vein,
treating the three Fischer groups in pursuit of the deﬁnitive proof of existence
and uniqueness. Neither of these books is comprehensive, as all aspects of the
groups which are not explicitly required are omitted.
The two volumes on ‘Geometry of sporadic groups’ by Ivanov, and by
Ivanov and Shpectorov [53, 54] are more specialised, though easier to read.
Ivanov’s recent book ‘J 4’ is slower-paced, as it is designed to be introductory,
and includes some useful background material, but, disappointingly, makes no
attempt to be comprehensive, and leaves out much that is known about the
group.
For background on coding theory, see for example Ray Hill’s book ‘A ﬁrst
course in coding theory’ [50], which contains among other things an alternative
construction of the binary Golay codes. There are numerous other books on
coding theory, many of which mention the Golay codes.
